    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p46-q71-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Explain why $P(x-2)=0$ has roots $\\alpha+2$, $\\beta+2$, $\\gamma+2$ and $\\delta+2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The graph of $y=P(x-2)$ is the graph of $y=P(x)$ translated 2 units to the right, so each root increases by 2.",
          "worked": "Replacing $x$ with $x-2$ translates the graph 2 units to the right. For example, $P((\\alpha+2)-2)=P(\\alpha)=0$. The same holds for the other three roots, giving $\\alpha+2$, $\\beta+2$, $\\gamma+2$ and $\\delta+2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p46-q71-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, find the value of\n$$(\\alpha+2)(\\beta+2)(\\gamma+2)(\\delta+2).$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$-50$.\n\nThe shifted polynomial is monic of degree 4, so its product of roots equals its constant term $P(-2)$.",
          "worked": "The polynomial $P(x-2)$ is monic of degree 4. Its product of roots equals its constant term, found by setting $x=0$:\n$$\\begin{align*}(\\alpha+2)(\\beta+2)(\\gamma+2)(\\delta+2)&=P(-2)\\\\(\\alpha+2)(\\beta+2)(\\gamma+2)(\\delta+2)&=(-2)^4-15(-2)^2+10(-2)+14\\\\(\\alpha+2)(\\beta+2)(\\gamma+2)(\\delta+2)&=16-60-20+14\\\\(\\alpha+2)(\\beta+2)(\\gamma+2)(\\delta+2)&=-50.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      }
    ]
  },
